-\paragraph{Identity replacement follow-up: details (exploratory).} The two searched winners can differ in every knob, not only $x$. With every other knob held fixed, switching replacement off in the D2+H3 winner lowers the gain by \site{rs_fx_b3} against B3, \site{rs_fx_r3} against R3, and \site{rs_fx_c} against C$^*$, and switching it on in the D2 winner raises the gain by only \site{rs_rev_b3}, \site{rs_rev_r3}, and \site{rs_rev_c}. No D2 winner replaces its identity. The truthful references can: \site{rs_ref_any} of the \site{rs_ref_n} frozen references replace an identity on the evaluation seeds. Each rule's reference cancels in $I$ but not in $G$. As a sensitivity calculation, we measured on the author's development machine (arm64) each frozen reference's profit with replacement and with replacement disabled, and added the signed difference to the stored Linux gains; the adjustment can increase or decrease a gain, and it is not a re-evaluation with non-replacing references. The largest absolute difference is \site{rs_ref_max}; the adjustment moves G(D2+H3) and G(D2) by the same amount, less than \const{0.001}, leaves $S$ at \site{rs_ref_s}, and moves the C$^*$ minus R3 difference by less than \const{0.002}. The Mac reproduces the stored reference profits only on average, with a largest absolute mean discrepancy of about \site{rs_ref_val}, close to the largest replacement difference. $S$ is a ratio of two searched gaps and carries their search error (Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset}). \site{rs_flag} of the \site{rs_flag_n} D2 doubling checks are flagged (largest change \site{rs_flag_max}), and on fresh0 the D2 gap moves from \site{rs_f0_g0} to \site{rs_f0_g0_d} under doubling; an unflagged cell is not a converged one. In two frozen comparisons the evaluated D2+H3 winner earns less than the evaluated D2 winner: $I$ is \site{rs_nest_c} for C$^*$ on fresh3 and \site{rs_nest_r} for R3 on fresh4. These reversals can reflect finite search or differences between training and evaluation outcomes; they disappear under doubling, which does not establish convergence. We keep the frozen searches as primary and report the doubled results as sensitivity.
