    20	\begin{abstract}
    21	% BEGIN-EV2
    22	A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity; under uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement, free replacement makes the ban costless, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. We study R3, a rule that audits new identities first and then keeps targeting the newest ones, in two registered simulation tests; the second was locked before six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash. Without honest identity turnover, the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison of R3 with uniform audits (B3) passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six markets under the prescribed searches, all passes qualified by search flags, while R3 lowers the attacker's measured gain relative to B3 on every market; R3 and a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, tested in the second test only, are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{abs_q1_est}). Exploratory follow-ups suggest that this advantage erodes when honest providers also replace their identities: R3's audits move to honest newcomers, and the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{abs_ch_fa1} to \site{abs_ch_fa5} between churn rates of $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} per round. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, planned and timestamped before they were drawn, met \site{abs_rp_met} of its five expectations with its primary search winners (\site{abs_rp_fmet} with the all-frozen winners); with the primary winners its mean changes rest mainly on two markets, and its market-level intervals mostly include zero.
    23	% END-EV2
    24	\end{abstract}
