   353	\paragraph{Identity replacement follow-up: intervals, the Q1 contrast, and expectations (exploratory).} The D2 gap's paired-seed interval excludes zero conditional on these markets and selected policies, whereas the exploratory $t$ interval of the same D2 gap across the six market means, [\site{rs_gd0_t_lo}, \site{rs_gd0_t_hi}], includes zero; the gap is positive on \site{rs_gd0_pos} of the six markets (\site{rs_gd0_pos_d} with the doubled searches), and most of its negative value comes from fresh5 (without fresh5, $S$ is \site{rs_s_loo5}). The seed intervals above hold the six markets fixed; leaving out one market at a time gives $S$ from \site{rs_loo_lo} to \site{rs_loo_hi} over both search versions, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset} lists the gaps per market. The same holds for the Q1 contrast: the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 gain difference is \site{rs_cr1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_cr0} under D2 ($t$ interval [\site{rs_cr0_lo}, \site{rs_cr0_hi}]; doubled searches [\site{rs_cr0_dlo}, \site{rs_cr0_dhi}]). The difference between the two prescribed searches, $I = g(\mathrm{D2{+}H3}) - g(\mathrm{D2})$, is \site{rs_i_b3} against B3, \site{rs_i_r3} against R3, and \site{rs_i_c} against C$^*$; it does not identify the optimal value of replacement. The expectations set in advance score as follows. $I(\mathrm{B3}) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_b}'' (\site{rs_sign_b} markets with the frozen searches, \site{rs_sign_bd} with the doubled ones). $I(\mathrm{C}^*) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_c}'' (\site{rs_sign_c} and \site{rs_sign_cd} markets; fresh3 gives \site{rs_f3_c} with the frozen searches). Pooled $S > \const{0.5}$: ``\site{rs_exp_s}''.
   354	
   355	\paragraph{Identity replacement follow-up: details (exploratory).} The two searched winners can differ in every knob, not only $x$. With every other knob held fixed, switching replacement off in the D2+H3 winner lowers the gain by \site{rs_fx_b3} against B3, \site{rs_fx_r3} against R3, and \site{rs_fx_c} against C$^*$, and switching it on in the D2 winner raises the gain by only \site{rs_rev_b3}, \site{rs_rev_r3}, and \site{rs_rev_c}. No D2 winner replaces its identity. The truthful references can: \site{rs_ref_any} of the \site{rs_ref_n} frozen references replace an identity on the evaluation seeds. Each rule's reference cancels in $I$ but not in $G$. As a sensitivity calculation, we measured on the author's development machine (arm64) each frozen reference's profit with replacement and with replacement disabled, and added the signed difference to the stored Linux gains; the adjustment can increase or decrease a gain, and it is not a re-evaluation with non-replacing references. The largest absolute difference is \site{rs_ref_max}; the adjustment moves G(D2+H3) and G(D2) by the same amount, less than \const{0.001}, leaves $S$ at \site{rs_ref_s}, and moves the C$^*$ minus R3 difference by less than \const{0.002}. The development machine reproduces the stored reference profits only on average, with a largest absolute mean discrepancy of about \site{rs_ref_val}, close to the largest replacement difference. $S$ is a ratio of two searched gaps and carries their search error (Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset}). \site{rs_flag} of the \site{rs_flag_n} D2 doubling checks are flagged (largest change \site{rs_flag_max}), and on fresh0 the D2 gap moves from \site{rs_f0_g0} to \site{rs_f0_g0_d} under doubling; an unflagged cell is not a converged one. In two frozen comparisons the evaluated D2+H3 winner earns less than the evaluated D2 winner: $I$ is \site{rs_nest_c} for C$^*$ on fresh3 and \site{rs_nest_r} for R3 on fresh4. These reversals can reflect finite search or differences between training and evaluation outcomes; they disappear under doubling, which does not establish convergence. We keep the frozen searches as primary and report the doubled results as sensitivity.
