   442	\FloatBarrier
   443	\section{Registration detail}\label{sec:app-design}
   444	\paragraph{Calibration.} A multiplier $\kappa$ replaces each nominal tail $\alpha_e$ by $\kappa\alpha_e$, so $\kappa < \const{1}$ widens the intervals. It was selected from $\{1, 1/2, 1/4\}$ on fixtures built from frozen development attacks and references on the headline market and dev0 (pools of \const{20000} seeds per market; Q1 in six equal strata), using \const{10000} selection replications, and admitted on \const{20000} independent validation replications if every endpoint's Clopper--Pearson upper bound on its miss rate, at confidence $1 - \const{0.01}/m$ with $m = \const{8}$ endpoints for Q1 and $m = \const{36}$ for Q4 (the two-market branch evaluates \const{24} Q4 endpoints and keeps the denominator \const{36}, which is conservative), was at most its allocated tail. The fixtures hold the policies and references fixed, so admission does not cover search or reference selection, and it does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule distributions. Q1's categories follow the logic of equivalence testing and Q4's market test is an intersection-union test~\cite{berger1996bioequivalence}; the calibration follows the reporting of simulation studies of statistical methods~\cite{morris2019simulation}. Conditional on valid calibrated component $p$-values, Holm controls the Q1 direction family and the six Q4 joint gain-and-efficiency decisions separately; fixture admission does not establish that validity on the fresh-market distributions. Q1's reported intervals use calibrated Bonferroni tails, and its efficiency condition uses a separate nominal one-sided \const{97.5} percent bound. The report stores the calibrated two-sided Q1 value that enters Holm and, for Q4, the raw one-sided bootstrap $p$-values of the two components and the calibrated market value $\max(p'_{\mathrm{gain}}, p'_{\mathrm{eff}})$ with $p' = \min(\const{1}, p/\kappa)$; Tables~\ref{tab:q1} and~\ref{tab:q4} print these with their Holm-adjusted values, and an audit script recomputes these values from the records and reproduces each Q1 category and direction rejection and each Q4 decision.
   445	
   446	\paragraph{Development.} All choices before the draw were development, on the headline market and the development instance dev0 (branch \texttt{two20k}): a benchmark priced every cell; the comparator was selected and the tuned variants were tuned; the defenders were frozen at a signed commit; a fresh session of another model (gpt-6.1-sol), started by the author, wrote H4 against the frozen defenders; after a machine restart the session that ran the H4 search resumed at the author's instruction, without reading its log or bundle, committed the bundle in the author's separate clone, and only its hash entered the protocol; a coverage check on frozen development attacks admitted calibration multipliers $\kappa = \const{0.5}$ for Q1 and $\kappa = \const{0.25}$ for Q4; and the predictions were written and hashed. Budget caps were \const{12} USD for development and \const{40} USD for evaluation, within a written authorisation of \const{52} USD. The evaluation cap forced three registered cuts: the tuned variants, H4, and the D2 extras run on the headline market only.
   447	
   448	\paragraph{Deviations from the protocol.} Two arose during the evaluation. First, the runner recalibrates each fresh market where it runs, and the evaluation ran on Linux x86 machines, while the registered instance file holds the calibration computed on the author's arm64 machine. In each fresh market, between \const{1} and \const{6} provider parameters differ between the two in the last binary digit (one unit in the last place, a relative difference near $10^{\const{-16}}$); the headline market is unaffected. The runner's verification compares configuration hashes exactly and refuses these records. We kept them: a container on the evaluation platform reproduces each run's configuration hash, every differing value is within one unit in the last place of the registered one, and verification passes on every other check when given the reproduced configuration. The check is a script in the artifact. It establishes that the configurations match, not that the decisions are unchanged, since small input differences can change discrete simulation branches; the re-evaluation below holds the selected attacks and references fixed and does not repeat search or reference selection under the registered configurations. We therefore re-evaluated the frozen policies of every fresh-market cell that Q1 and Q4 use on the registration platform with the registered configurations, against the frozen references: per-seed gains change in every cell, cell means move by less than one hundredth, and every Q1 and Q4 decision is unchanged (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/stability.py}; Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability}). The report's addendum lists the hashes, states the interval labels, and traces search-flag dependencies through each cell's references, which qualifies three further decisions as about the prescribed search procedure. Second, the fresh-market runs cost about ten percent more than estimated, and the author raised the evaluation cap from \const{40} to \const{43} USD and the written authorisation from \const{52} to \const{55} USD so that every registered cell could run; no analysis changed. The exploratory follow-ups after the report ran under a separate follow-up cap of \const{72} USD (program limit \const{123} USD, the author's approval of 12 November 2026). Recorded spend: identity-replacement follow-up \const{1.65} USD; churn at $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, \const{4.99} USD; churn at \const{0.02} and \const{0.03}, at most \const{7.70} USD; runner-up comparator, at most \const{3.14} USD; continued searches, \const{2.76} USD for a first attempt that lost its results to a killed worker and \const{3.40} USD for the rerun; prospective-exploratory replication, \const{26.45} USD in three pod runs (\const{11.13}, \const{0.02} for a launch whose upload failed, and \const{15.30}), within its own cap of \const{33} USD (the two upper bounds are ceiling estimates; the others are billed amounts or balance differences). The replication's run code computed no diagnostics for continued R3 winners at $p = \const{0.03}$, a gap against its plan (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-rep}).
   449	
   450	\FloatBarrier
   451	\section{Exact but costly checks}\label{sec:app-exact}
   452	The model's audit already reveals delivered quality exactly, at a cost charged to the operator's budget. An execution proof such as FlashProve checks a different object, that a committed computation ran, and does not remove delivery noise. It fits the model's audit cost only if the operator funds both proving and verification; provider-funded proving would need separate incentive accounting. Either way, which jobs to check and how to sanction remain the questions; if the cost of a proof fell close to that of the computation itself, the case for sampled audits would weaken. This discussion is outside the registered tests and changes none of their claims.
   453	
   454	\FloatBarrier
   455	\section{Parameters and definitions}\label{sec:app-params}\label{app:params}
   456	First test and headline market: providers $i = 1, \dots, 10$ have $q^{\max}_i = (0.60, 0.65, \dots, 1.00, 1.00)$, $b_i = (0.40, 0.38, \dots, 0.22)$, $a_i = 0.02$, $k_i = 6$, $[v_{\min}, v_{\max}] = [0.6, 1.4]$, $\sigma = 0.05$, $\beta = 0.05$, $\rho = 0.2$. Fresh markets of the second test: $N$, $J$, $T$, $\sigma$, $\beta$, $\rho$, and $v_{\min}$ as at the headline; $q^{\max}$ ten sorted draws from $U[0.55, 1.0]$; $b_i$ falling linearly in the quality rank between $b_{\mathrm{hi}} \sim U[0.34, 0.46]$ and $b_{\mathrm{lo}} \sim U[0.18, 0.26]$ with noise $U[-0.02, 0.02]$; $a_i \sim U[0, 0.05]$; $k_i$ uniform in $\{4, \dots, 8\}$; $v_{\max} \sim U[1.2, 1.6]$. The focal provider is at the median position. The first-best surplus $S^*$ of a seed is the sum over rounds of the maximum-weight assignment of jobs to capacity slots at full effort.
