FIRST PAGE
               Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:
                  Registered Tests of Age-Ranked Audits
              and an Exploratory Limit Under Honest Churn

                                             Ömer Demirel

                                             November 2026


                                                 Abstract
         A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider
     can return at once under a new identity. We show that under uniform audits with refund-and-
     ban enforcement free replacement makes the ban costless, given honest rivals whose play does
     not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits new identities first and then
     keeps targeting the newest ones. In two registered simulation tests, the second locked before
     six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash, we compare R3 at matched budget
     and sanctions with uniform audits (B3) and, in the second test, a verified-history comparator,
     C∗ . Without honest identity turnover, R3 lowers the attacker’s measured gain relative to B3
     on every fresh market (the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on 3 of six), and R3
     and C∗ are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” within a pre-set margin (C∗ minus R3: 0.021); in
     an exploratory comparison without churn, the development runner-up, a second verified-history
     comparator, C-thr(12), shows no consistent direction against R3. At the headline market, the
     selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower all three rules’ gross efficiency to about 0.840 while
     earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C∗ . Exploratory follow-ups after the
     registered report find that, within the prescribed searches, disabling the attacker’s identity
     replacement removes most of R3’s measured advantage over B3, and that the advantage erodes
     when honest providers also replace their identities. R3’s audits then move to honest newcomers:
     with the frozen searches, the share of the attacker’s jobs it audits falls from 0.366 to 0.042
     between churn rates of p = 0.01 and 0.05 per round, and at p = 0.03 and 0.05 both C∗ minus R3
     and B3 minus R3 are lower than without churn on every sampled market. At p = 0.05, C∗ minus
     R3 is −0.173 and B3 minus R3 is −0.052 (−0.033 with the doubled searches). These follow-ups
     rest on six markets, fixed parameters, illustrative churn rates, one comparator under churn, and
     searches of which 21 of 72 are flagged incomplete; their market-level intervals mostly include
     zero. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, planned and timestamped before
     they were drawn and running C-thr(12) under churn as well, met 5 of its five expectations with
     its primary search winners (4 with the all-frozen winners); with the primary winners its mean
     changes rest mainly on two of the six markets, and its market-level intervals mostly include zero.


1    Introduction
A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost
and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are
expensive and run after the response is served. FlashProve, for example, proves that a committed
integer model produced a given token under greedy decoding; the provider generates proofs after the
response is served, at about 70 to 95 times the cost of one forward pass [27, p. 18], for a window of


                                                     1

UNRESOLVED 0
PAGE 24 
 draw, run records, analysis, and the record digest that its paper extract is checked against,
and the paper sources. One command (docs/revision/deviation/reproduce_deviation.py)
checks the artifact against the tag and reproduces the second test’s report under the disclosed
configuration substitution; the paper’s second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the
tagged report, the disclosed stability output, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs
by the scripts in docs/revision/paper-v2, docs/revision/posthoc, docs/revision/audit,
docs/revision/followup2, and docs/revision/followup3, and the first test’s records and
verdict are in the release as recorded in October 2026.


Use of AI tools
AI tools were used throughout this work; responsibility for all of it rests with the author. AI coding
assistants (Anthropic’s Claude models) wrote most of the simulator, analysis, test and release code
to the author’s specifications, and drafted parts of the text; the author set the research questions, the
registered hypotheses, decision rules and every amendment, and checked and revised all text. Two
attackers involve language models by design: H1 is a language model acting as the focal provider,
and H4 was written by a fresh session of an OpenAI model, started by the author, against the frozen
defenders (Appendix D). Drafts of the protocol, code and paper were reviewed by OpenAI GPT
models and by Claude, read-only; each correction was checked against the repository before it was
adopted. No AI tool is an author.


A     Further registered results and exploratory details
Which parts of R3 matter (Q2). Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the
fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are −0.083 for the priority phase P and −0.095 for newest-
first residual targeting G, both reductions, and 0.004 for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: 0.005).
The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G’s
