   257	\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
   258	% BEGIN-EV2
   259	For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets (under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule's strongest family, it is positive on all six), and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. Against R3 and C$^*$, the registered W1 searches selected policies with zero estimated gain and gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}; exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. In an exploratory follow-up, most of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed searches disappears when attacker replacement is disabled: under the D2 family, neither pooled mean gap (B3 minus R3, or C$^*$ minus R3) is distinguishable from zero using exploratory market-level $t$ intervals. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers, the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{c_ch_fa1} to \site{c_ch_fa5} between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, and the pooled point estimates of its advantage turn negative: at $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{c_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{c_ch_q5} (\site{c_ch_q5d} with the doubled searches; the frozen value includes one search-flagged cell with a large improvement under doubling). Against C$^*$, the one verified-history comparator run under churn in that follow-up, the comparison is unresolved at $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, and at \const{0.03} C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on every market under both search versions; against uniform audits the pooled point estimate of its advantage stays positive through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not at \const{0.05}. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, fixed and timestamped before they were drawn, finds with its primary winners the same deterioration from no churn against C$^*$, the runner-up comparator, and uniform audits under its descriptive criterion; at $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are negative on \site{c_rp_dn} and \site{c_rp_qn} of the new markets, but at \const{0.03} C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on only \site{c_rp_d3n}, the mean changes rest mainly on two markets, and the market-level intervals mostly include zero. The registered Q1 and Q4 inferences use calibration admitted on development fixtures; the follow-up intervals are exploratory: seed-bootstrap intervals condition on the six sampled markets, while market-level $t$ intervals treat those markets as a sample; both hold the selected attacks and references fixed and omit search uncertainty, and the doubled searches are sensitivity analyses. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, is released with the paper (see Availability).
   260	% END-EV2
   261	
   262	\paragraph{Availability.} The repository is public at \url{https://github.com/demirelo/touchstone-registered-tests} (release v2.1, archived at \url{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24498973}), and its eight record files, with their SHA-\const{256} hashes, are archived at \url{https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24499068}. The release identifies the frozen evaluation code by the signed tag \texttt{protocol-v2} and preserves the original tagged-code report. It contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, and the deviation evidence, and, separately dated, the decision-stability re-evaluation, the exploratory post hoc analyses with the per-seed evaluation and training inputs of the lottery analysis, the prediction-scoring correction with its regression test, the inference audit, the identity-replacement and churn follow-ups with their run records, the prospective-exploratory replication with its timestamped plan and manifest, block-hash draw, run records, analysis, and the record digest that its paper extract is checked against, and the paper sources. One command (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/reproduce\_deviation.py}) checks the artifact against the tag and reproduces the second test's report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the paper's second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the tagged report, the disclosed stability output, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs by the scripts in \texttt{docs/revision/paper-v2}, \texttt{docs/revision/posthoc}, \texttt{docs/revision/audit}, \texttt{docs/revision/followup2}, and \texttt{docs/revision/followup3}, and the first test's records and verdict are in the release as recorded in October~\const{2026}.
   263	
   264	\section*{Use of AI tools}\label{sec:ai-use}
   265	AI tools were used throughout this work; responsibility for all of it rests with the author. AI coding assistants (Anthropic's Claude models) wrote most of the simulator, analysis, test and release code to the author's specifications, and drafted parts of the text; the author set the research questions, the registered hypotheses, decision rules and every amendment, and checked and revised all text. Two attackers involve language models by design: H1 is a language model acting as the focal provider, and H4 was written by a fresh session of an OpenAI model, started by the author, against the frozen defenders (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-design}). Drafts of the protocol, code and paper were reviewed by OpenAI GPT models and by Claude, read-only; each correction was checked against the repository before it was adopted. No AI tool is an author.
   266	
# Design review, round 1 (read-only; no paid APIs; do not read `sealed/`; reasoning effort high; under 1,500 words)

You review the design of the revision program for the Touchstone paper: `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-22-revision-program-design.md`. The author chose the full program after an external review (triage: `docs/paper/gpt-pro-review-triage-2026-10-22.md`, section "Program"). Context: paper `paper/main.tex` (draft 8), final evaluation `docs/final-evaluation-2026-10-20/README.md`, robustness `docs/robustness/2026-10-22-results.md`, theory `docs/theory/reset-deterrence.md`, simulator spec `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-14-touchstone-simulator-design.md` (admissible class section 4.0), code in `sim/` and `scripts/` (R3: `sim/mechanisms/proposed.py`; B3: `sim/mechanisms/second_score.py`, `audit_random.py`; tuning: `sim/tuning.py`; attackers: `sim/policies/exploratory.py`, `scripts/explore_attackers.py`; robustness runner `scripts/robustness_v1.py`; killed predecessor `git show 46694cb:sim/mechanisms/proposed.py`).

Judge whether this design, once executed, answers the four questions of its section 1 at the standard of a strong AAMAS or GameSec main-track paper, and whether it is internally sound. Check with evidence (file:line, primary sources for Sarmenta 2001 and BOINC adaptive replication):
1. The credibility comparator is a fair, matched, and strong form of the established principle inside the admissible class.
2. The factorial decomposition identifies the components' contributions; the equivalence tests are correct and sufficient.
3. The common tuning procedure is equal across rules and does not favour R3 or R4.
4. The attack families and objectives are strong enough; X7-SC uses only allowed observations; the welfare and coalition objectives are well defined.
5. The generator, the block-hash randomness, the seed ranges, and the lock procedure prevent outcome-dependent choices.
6. The decision rules and the interval method are valid for the estimands (pairing, multiplicity, pooling over 6 instances, the 5-of-6 rule).
7. Cost: which cells to cut, if any, to stay within the caps without weakening Q1 to Q3.
8. Answer the seven questions of section 11.

Output exactly:
VERDICT: accept | accept-with-caveat | reject
CORRECTIONS: numbered, each with the section, the exact change, and the reason with evidence.
ANSWERS: (1) to (7).
CONFIDENCE: 0 to 1.
# Protocol v2 review, round 1 (read-only; no paid APIs; do not read `sealed/`, `sealed2/`, or `~/touchstone-h4`; reasoning effort high; under 1,500 words)

Review the protocol before the lock: `experiments/protocol-v2.yaml` (written by `scripts/lock_v2.py --write`, `--check` ok) and `docs/revision/predictions-v2.md`. Design: `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-22-revision-program-design.md` (draft 7, accepted); operations plan: `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-10-25-revision-operations.md`; the record of what was done: `docs/revision/operations-ledger.md`, `docs/revision/lessons.md`, `docs/revision/h4-decision-record/`, `experiments/v2/` (promoted inputs). Code commit 627a2de; this branch adds only data and documents since then.

Check with evidence:
1. Every bound value follows from its source (hashes, branch two20k, cuts 3, kappa Q1 0.5 and Q4 0.25 from `kappa_effective`, the H4 commitment, the cost, the approval sentence); run `uv run python scripts/lock_v2.py --check` if useful.
2. The departures from the plan, each recorded in the ledger. Say for each whether it compromises a confirmatory claim, and what must change before the lock if so:
   a. The executor (not the author) launched the H4 session resume; the bundle was not read; the executor committed it in the author's clone (`git add`/`commit` only) because the session's sandbox made `.git` read-only, and recomputed the bundle hash (equal to the session's export record). The protocol binds the clone commit aa28ed4; the export record's `commit` field is c5227a0 (the HEAD at check time, before the commit).
   b. Coverage (Operation 4) ran before the decision after the H4 timing. That decision first stopped (development 12.73 USD) because coverage was counted as spent and as remaining work; it was recomputed on the ledger as it stood before coverage (`docs/revision/h4-decision-record/`), giving cuts 3 and total 49.16 USD. The actual development spend is 11.42 USD (cap 12).
   c. The coverage pools were built by an orchestration driver (`results/pods/cov_driver.py`, quoted in the ledger) that calls the frozen `sim.calibration_v2.build_pool` on 25-seed slices, because the frozen `build_market_pool` exceeded pod memory at 20,000 seeds; its pool file was byte-identical to the frozen builder's on a 40-seed pool. The dev0 pool was rescued on its pod by fetching git history into a shallow clone (code unchanged).
   d. Tuning ran B3-t and R3-t in parallel with the comparator selection.
3. The predictions: are they derived from development evidence only, correctly quoted, and stated so that the report can score them? Note any prediction that the cited evidence does not support, or that a decision rule makes unscorable.
4. Anything that must be fixed before the author signs, stamps, and tags `protocol-v2`.

Output exactly:
VERDICT: accept | accept-with-caveat | reject
CORRECTIONS: numbered, each with the file, the exact change, and the reason with evidence.
CONFIDENCE: 0 to 1.

