22:A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. We show that under uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement free replacement makes the ban costless, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits new identities first and then keeps targeting the newest ones. In two registered simulation tests, the second locked before six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash, we compare R3 at matched budget and sanctions with uniform audits (B3) and, in the second test, a verified-history comparator, C$^*$. Without honest identity turnover, R3 lowers the attacker's measured gain relative to B3 on every fresh market (the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six), and R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{abs_q1_est}); in an exploratory comparison without churn, the development runner-up, a second verified-history comparator, C-thr(\const{12}), shows no consistent direction against R3. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0} while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$.
23:Exploratory follow-ups after the registered report find that, within the prescribed searches, disabling the attacker's identity replacement removes most of R3's measured advantage over B3, and that the advantage erodes when honest providers also replace their identities. R3's audits then move to honest newcomers: with the frozen searches, the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{abs_ch_fa1} to \site{abs_ch_fa5} between churn rates of $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} per round, and at $p = \const{0.03}$ and \const{0.05} both C$^*$ minus R3 and B3 minus R3 are lower than without churn on every sampled market. At $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{abs_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{abs_ch_q5} (\site{abs_ch_q5d} with the doubled searches). These follow-ups rest on six markets, fixed parameters, illustrative churn rates, one comparator under churn, and searches of which \site{abs_ch_inc} of \site{abs_ch_cells} are flagged incomplete; their market-level intervals mostly include zero. A prospective-exploratory replication on six new markets, planned and timestamped before they were drawn and running C-thr(\const{12}) under churn as well, met \site{abs_rp_met} of its five expectations with its primary search winners (\site{abs_rp_fmet} with the all-frozen winners); with the primary winners its mean changes rest mainly on two of the six markets, and its market-level intervals mostly include zero.
39:\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless under uniform audits (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}), and a bound on the audits that age-ranked targeting gives a replacement identity (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}). (ii) Two registered tests. Without honest churn, the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison of R3 with uniform audits passes on three of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; the prescribed searches give R3 a lower measured attacker gain than uniform audits on every fresh market, and they classify R3 and a development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent within the pre-set materiality margin; R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other. (iii) Exploratory follow-ups, specified after the second test's registered report. Within the prescribed D2-family searches, disabling the attacker's identity replacement removes most of R3's estimated gap to uniform audits; the separately searched winners can differ in every knob, so this does not isolate the value of replacement. Under honest churn, at the tested rates, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers while an attacker that keeps its identity is rarely audited, and its measured advantage over the comparator is unresolved at churn rates of \const{0.01} and \const{0.02} per round and negative on all six markets at \const{0.03} under both search versions, while the pooled point estimate of its advantage over uniform audits stays positive through \const{0.03} and is negative at \const{0.05}. Two further follow-ups continue the flagged searches and add the development runner-up comparator without churn. A prospective-exploratory replication, planned and timestamped before six new markets were drawn, meets all of its expectations with its primary search winners and all but one with the all-frozen winners: under a descriptive criterion, R3's position against both verified-history comparators and uniform audits worsens from no churn, but with the primary winners the mean changes rest mainly on two markets, the comparison with C$^*$ at the middle rate is mixed across the new markets, and the market-level intervals mostly include zero. (iv) A record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the failed predictions, and the follow-ups' plans, released with the paper (see Availability).
73:\paragraph{Benchmark and metrics.} One focal provider deviates while the others play honestly: full effort $q_i = q^{\max}_i$, the truthful claim $c_i = q^{\max}_i$, and a bid equal to their delivery cost $a_i + b_i q^{\max}_i$; this is a unilateral deviation, not an equilibrium. For a seed with first-best surplus $S^*$, the focal provider's \emph{gain} is its profit under the attack minus its profit under a selected truthful reference, divided by $S^*/N$. The truthful reference is the policy with the highest mean normalised training profit in the rule's menu of truthful candidate policies; it need not be full-effort play and may replace identities. \emph{Gross efficiency} is $(W + \text{audit cost})/S^*$, with $W$ the realised social surplus net of audit cost; net efficiency is $W/S^*$. Gain is a difference of two searched values, so search error can bias it either way. It is excess profit when replacement is available; it does not isolate how much of that profit comes from the ability to replace an identity; Section~\ref{sec:reset} reports an exploratory matched comparison with replacement disabled, within the D2 family only. The second test adds two objectives at its headline market (the first test's market; Appendix~\ref{sec:app-params}): welfare attacks that minimise gross efficiency, without a constraint (W0) or subject to a nonnegative mean normalised gain over the attacker's reference on the training seeds (W1), whose profitability is then called supported only if its nominal 95\% lower bound on the evaluation seeds is nonnegative; and coalitions of two or five providers, scored by the total gain $G_{\mathrm{tot}}$ and by the worst member's mean gain $G_{\min}$, both against a coordinated truthful reference. Coalition results say nothing about coalition stability.
86:\section{What the theory shows}\label{sec:theory}
164:\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC; the mean gains are \site{r_abs_cs} against C$^*$ and \site{r_abs_r3} against R3 under D2+H3, and \site{r_abs_cs_x} and \site{r_abs_r3_x} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator; the margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ is half the first test's comparative margin, not an estimated threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table~\ref{tab:ph-doubled}, exploratory) changes fresh3's difference from \site{r_dbl_f3} to \site{r_dbl_f3_d}, and the pooled differences to \site{r_dbl_pool_d} under D2+H3 and \site{r_dbl_pool_x} under X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. Two exploratory follow-ups after the report bear on this comparison. Continued searches of \site{r_rb_cells} registered cells, \site{r_rb_flagged} whose doubling check was flagged and \site{r_rb_unflagged} unflagged X7-SC cells with held-out shortfalls (up to three rounds each), all passed the incremental-improvement check within three rounds, which bounds neither global convergence nor the remaining search error; they included no churn cells; with their final winners substituted, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{r_rb_q1} under D2+H3 and B3 minus R3 is \site{r_rb_q4}, and the largest change of a market difference is \site{r_rb_max} (fresh3, where the difference becomes positive). The development runner-up comparator, C-thr(\const{12}), evaluated on the same six markets without churn, gives the attacker a pooled D2+H3 gain lower than against R3 (runner-up minus R3: \site{r_ra_est}, [\site{r_ra_lo}, \site{r_ra_hi}]; ``\site{r_ra_cat}'', a label from nominal percentile intervals with the registered margin, not the registered calibration and Holm procedure); R3 has the lower gain on \site{r_ra_pos} of six markets, and \site{r_ra_minm} (\site{r_ra_min}) contributes most of the frozen pooled difference. Table~\ref{tab:ph-runnerup} gives all four family and search versions: every market-level $t$ interval includes zero, R3 has the lower gain on four or five of six markets, and C$^*$ minus runner-up is positive in all four; \site{r_ru_flag} of the \site{r_ru_flag_n} D2+H3 runner-up doubling checks and \site{r_ru_flag_x} of \site{r_ru_flag_xn} under X7-SC are flagged incomplete. The registered direction holds for C$^*$, not for verified-history targeting in general. The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six fixed markets. Treating the six markets as a sample of the neighbourhood (exploratory), a $t$ interval over the per-market differences is [\site{r_ml_lo}, \site{r_ml_hi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_pos} of six markets positive, and [\site{r_ml_xlo}, \site{r_ml_xhi}] under X7-SC; with the doubled-search winners it is [\site{r_ml_dlo}, \site{r_ml_dhi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_dpos} positive. With each rule's strongest training-selected family the interval is [\site{r_env_lo}, \site{r_env_hi}], with \site{r_env_pos} of six markets positive. The direction is therefore fairly stable across these analyses, while the size is not: every upper end is near the margin, so the equivalence label is the most fragile part; beyond these six markets the sign is not established either, since the frozen interval contains zero and the searches share inputs; on these markets the registered direction tests reject zero for the prescribed searches, and another comparator would be a different comparison. The frozen D2+H3 interval contains zero and crosses the margin, so this analysis does not establish the category for the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside the margin, but that family mostly repeats D2+H3 (below). In relative terms, under D2+H3, the attacker's pooled mean gain against C$^*$ exceeds that against R3 by a fraction of \site{r_rel}. The two Q1 families are close to one test: X7-SC's search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner, and in \site{r_x7_same} of the \site{r_x7_cells} Q1 cells it selected that winner, with identical evaluated gains; only fresh3 against C$^*$ and fresh4 against R3 differ. With the headline market, the same holds in \site{r_x7_all} of \site{r_x7_all_n} market--rule cells, which is why most of prediction Q3-a's differences are exactly zero. With each rule's strongest training-selected family instead (Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope}), the pooled difference is \site{r_env_pool}, and \site{r_env_f1} on fresh1, where X5-AG/v2 is C$^*$'s strongest family. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain than C$^*$ (Section~\ref{sec:rules}); a comparator selected on attacker gain could change the direction label. The two rules differ more in whom they burden than in the attacker's gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest}), an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50}: R3 audits \site{r_hon_r3_0} and \site{r_hon_r3_1} of the replacement's served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C$^*$ audits \site{r_hon_cs_0} and \site{r_hon_cs_1} (its lowest bin; \site{r_hon_cs_2} in the next), and B3 about \site{r_hon_b3_0}. Under R3 the replacement earns less per round (relative to C$^*$, a fraction of \site{r_hon_rel}; the registered diagnostic stores bin means, so no interval is available) and loses more receipts to refunds paid to buyers on false audit failures; R3 directs more of the fixed audit capacity to this provider, which does not show higher total audit spending. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
180:\section{Identity replacement and R3's measured advantage}\label{sec:reset}
192:In the registered markets every honest provider keeps one identity for the whole episode, so after the start the newest identities are mostly the attacker's. This section asks what remains of R3's advantage when honest providers also replace identities. It is an exploratory follow-up after the report and changes no registered decision. The last part of the section reports a replication on new markets that was planned and timestamped before they were drawn. In both the follow-up and the replication, seed-bootstrap intervals condition on the six markets, market-level $t$ intervals use variation across those markets, and both condition on the selected search outputs and omit search uncertainty.
202:\paragraph{Where R3's audits go.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit} shows how each rule spends its audits under its selected attack. Under R3 the share of the attacker's jobs that is audited falls from \site{ch_fa_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_fa_2}, \site{ch_fa_3}, and \site{ch_fa_5}, while under C$^*$ it moves from \site{ch_fa_c1} to \site{ch_fa_c5} and under B3 it is \site{ch_fa_b5}. With the doubled searches R3's value at $p = \const{0.01}$ is \site{ch_fa_1d} (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}). At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{ch_yj_5} of the jobs and receive \site{ch_ya_5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{ch_est_5}. With the frozen searches, on two or three of the six markets, depending on the rate, R3's selected attack never replaces its identity (with the doubled searches, none at $p = \const{0.01}$; Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); there the attacker does not hide among the newcomers but stays old, and, once past its own priority window, is audited only from what the priority phase and newest-first targeting leave. Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} does not cover such an attacker, since it bounds the audits of a replacement identity. Where the selected attack does replace (\site{ch_rep_n} markets at $p = \const{0.05}$), R3 audits between \site{ch_rep_lo} and \site{ch_rep_hi} of its jobs with the frozen searches and between \site{ch_rep_lo_d} and \site{ch_rep_hi_d} with the doubled ones, and the attacker is uniquely the newest job-holding identity (by first offer, ties excluded) in only \site{ch_new_lo} to \site{ch_new_hi} of the rounds in which it holds jobs (frozen); where it stays old (the same \site{ch_stay_n} markets under both search versions), R3 audits between \site{ch_stay_lo} and \site{ch_stay_hi} of its jobs with the frozen searches and between \site{ch_stay_lo_d} and \site{ch_stay_hi_d} with the doubled ones. The measured share falls at each of the four sampled rates; four rates cannot show whether the decline is smooth or has a threshold. Audit share alone does not determine gain: R3's audit rate on the attacker falls below B3's between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, while the pooled B3 minus R3 gap stays positive through \const{0.03}. The diagnostics describe the setting; they do not isolate the cause, because churn also resets histories, ends bans, and changes the selected attacks. Consistent with them, the rise in R3's gain comes mainly from the attack's profit: in raw profit units it rises by \site{ch_pa_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$, while the reference's profit changes by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]).
204:\paragraph{Efficiency.} Under its selected attack, R3's gross efficiency falls from \site{ch_eff_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_eff_5} at \const{0.05} (\site{ch_eff_5d} with the doubled searches); C$^*$'s is \site{ch_eff_c5} at \const{0.05} and changes little across the rates (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); at \const{0.05} B3's is \site{ch_eff_b5} with the frozen and \site{ch_eff_b5d} with the doubled searches, and across the rates it shows no trend in $p$; it moves between rates on single markets where the selected attack differs. On \site{ch_eff_minm} at $p = \const{0.05}$, R3's efficiency under attack is \site{ch_eff_min}, below the registered threshold of \const{0.90}; this is an exploratory point estimate, not a registered failure. The registered efficiency condition is a no-churn result.
210:\paragraph{Replication results.} Table~\ref{tab:replication} gives the paired changes and the absolute contrasts, and Figure~\ref{fig:replication} shows the paired changes market by market; Appendix~\ref{sec:app-rep} gives them per market, with the attack and reference terms, the efficiency of every rule, and R3's audit allocation. With the primary winners all five expectations are met: the support criterion holds for C$^*$ minus R3 at $p = \const{0.05}$ (``\site{rp_e1}''), for B3 minus R3 at \const{0.05} (``\site{rp_e2}''), for both at \const{0.03} (``\site{rp_e3}''), and for the runner-up minus R3 at \const{0.05} (``\site{rp_e4}''), and R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs is lower at \const{0.05} than without churn on at least five of six markets (``\site{rp_e5}''). The mean paired changes are \site{rp_d5} ($d$), \site{rp_q5} ($q$), and \site{rp_u5} ($u$) at $p = \const{0.05}$, and \site{rp_d3}, \site{rp_q3}, and \site{rp_u3} at \const{0.03}. The material companion holds for \site{rp_mat} of the six contrast and rate pairs with the primary winners and for \site{rpf_mat} with the all-frozen winners; the support criterion holds for \site{rp_sup} and \site{rpf_sup}. These means rest mainly on two markets: at $p = \const{0.05}$ with the primary winners, $d$ changes by \site{rp_d5_t0} and \site{rp_d5_t1} on rep0 and rep1, and by between \site{rp_d5_rlo} and \site{rp_d5_rhi} on the other four; with the primary winners the other contrasts and the rate \const{0.03} show the same pattern (Table~\ref{tab:rep-markets}). The material companion therefore describes the mean change, not a typical market. With the primary winners, the only market $t$ interval of a mean change that excludes zero is that of B3 minus R3 at \const{0.05} ([\site{rp_q5_lo}, \site{rp_q5_hi}]); with the all-frozen winners the three intervals at \const{0.05} exclude zero and none at \const{0.03} does. With the all-frozen winners, the first, second, fourth, and fifth expectations are met (``\site{rpf_e1}'', ``\site{rpf_e2}'', ``\site{rpf_e4}'', ``\site{rpf_e5}''), and the third is ``\site{rpf_e3}'': C$^*$ minus R3 at \const{0.03} is lower than without churn on only \site{rpf_d3_k} of six markets, so it fails the support criterion while meeting the material companion. Continued search moved both the no-churn baselines and the churn cells: the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 difference with the primary winners minus that with the all-frozen winners is \site{rp_sd0} without churn, \site{rp_sd3} at $p = \const{0.03}$, and \site{rp_sd5} at \const{0.05}. With the all-frozen winners the mean paired changes at \const{0.05} are \site{rpf_d5} ($d$), \site{rpf_q5} ($q$), and \site{rpf_u5} ($u$), further from zero than with the primary winners: at this rate the better-searched winner set gives the weaker changes. The paired changes therefore carry the search uncertainty of the baselines as well as that of the churn cells. As planned, the failed expectation is reported without a causal reading.
219:\paragraph{Scope.} The earlier follow-up ran one verified-history comparator under churn; the replication adds the runner-up, and on the new markets the deterioration under its descriptive criterion is not specific to C$^*$. Every claim is restricted to the generator's market neighbourhood, the four rules, the D2+H3 family, illustrative churn rates, and the prescribed finite searches; neither the seed-bootstrap nor the market $t$ intervals cover search uncertainty. Within this model and at the tested rates, the pooled point estimates show no measured advantage of R3 over C$^*$: the comparison is unresolved at the two lowest churn rates of the earlier follow-up and negative from $p = \const{0.03}$; the pooled point estimate of R3's advantage over uniform audits stays positive through \const{0.03} and is negative at \const{0.05}. On the new markets the pooled point estimates agree, but at \const{0.03} the comparison with C$^*$ is mixed across markets.
