# Lessons, revision program (protocol v2)

Read this before any operations step. One entry per mistake: what happened, the guard that now prevents it, and the guard's kind (check > written rule > memory note). A repeated mistake means its guard failed: replace it with a stronger kind.

| Date | Mistake | Guard | Kind |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-10-28 | The pod tarball carried the laptop user's uid; git on the pod refused the repository ("dubious ownership") and all three tuning pods failed their prep check. | Build every pod tarball with `tar --uid 0 --gid 0`. Since 2026-11-02 every launcher refuses a tarball with a non-root member before creating a pod (tarball guard, below). | check (launcher) |
| 2026-10-28 | The launcher's seed push targets a remote directory that does not exist before the run; `comparator.json` never reached the C*-t pod and the run failed at start. | Put every input a pod run needs inside the tarball (under a git-ignored path if it is not tracked); do not use the seed argument. | rule |
| 2026-10-28 | A zsh loop used `set -- $t` to split a string; zsh does not word-split, so every budget reservation failed on its arguments. | Write budget and launch commands out explicitly, one per line; do not split strings into arguments in zsh. | rule |
| 2026-11-02 | The approval commit added a `.gitignore` line; `.gitignore` is outside the data-only paths, so every later commit failed `promote_v2.same_code` and coverage refused the checkout. | Restored `.gitignore` (91c80ff); the lock file is excluded through `.git/info/exclude`. A pre-commit hook refuses, on `claude/vigorous-villani-bbac79` and on any detached HEAD after `h4-base`, a staged path outside `experiments/v2/` and `docs/` (negative tests on the branch and on a detached HEAD: refused). | check (hook) |
| 2026-11-02 | The worktree's HEAD was detached after the ledger commit (unlabelled checkout in the reflog); a commit landed off the branch, and the first version of the hook skipped detached HEADs. | The hook now applies to detached HEADs after `h4-base`; check `git status -sb` before committing on this branch. | check (hook) + rule |
| 2026-10-28 | The H4 session command wrote only a mixed log; when the machine restarted, the session died and the final message had no file of its own. | The resume command uses `-o ~/h4-final.txt`; read only that file. | rule |
| 2026-11-02 | macOS `tar` wrote 709 hidden AppleDouble members (`._*`, not shown by its own listing); on the Linux pod they became fake `._*.json` tuning logs and both coverage pods failed at once (about 0.03 USD each). Tuning was unaffected (its code scans no folders). | `bench_pod.sh`, `tune_pod.sh`, `cov_pod.sh` refuse, before creating a pod, a tarball with any `._` member or non-root owner (Python `tarfile` count); negative test on the bad tarball: refused. Build with `COPYFILE_DISABLE=1 tar --no-mac-metadata --uid 0 --gid 0`. | check (launcher) |
| 2026-11-02 | The frozen `coverage_v2.build_market_pool` gives each worker one slice of all 20,000 seeds and `run_focal` keeps every episode record of a slice in memory; at full size the pods ran out of memory (`BrokenProcessPool`) on both markets. The 20-seed smoke could not show it; the benchmark priced coverage from per-episode time without a full-size run. | Before the first paid run of any producer, run it locally on at least 1 percent of its full size and check peak memory per worker (`/usr/bin/time -l`) scaled to the full size against the pod's RAM. Workaround for the frozen code: the orchestration driver `results/pods/cov_driver.py` calls the frozen `build_pool` on 25-seed slices; its pool file is byte-identical to the frozen builder's on a 40-seed pool. Code fix (bounded memory in `build_market_pool`) deferred to after the program: a code change now would invalidate the promoted tuning. | rule + equivalence check |
| 2026-11-02 | The coverage tarball was a shallow clone (`--depth 1`) of a data-only descendant; on the pod `same_code` could not prove the ancestry of 627a2de, so the frozen builder refused the finished headline pool after 46 minutes (about 0.45 USD lost). Earlier pods ran the code commit itself, which needs no history. | Launchers require `CODE_COMMIT` and refuse, before creating a pod, a tarball whose unpacked HEAD is not that commit or a provable data-only descendant (history guard; negative test on the shallow tarball: refused). Build tarballs from full clones. The driver checks `same_code` before any episode. | check (launcher) |
| 2026-11-02 | I edited `cov_pod.sh` while a launch was running it; bash reads a script as it executes, the running launch hit a syntax error after creating its pod, and its cleanup deleted the pod (seconds, no run). | Every launcher copies itself to `snap_<name>_<pid>.sh` at start and runs the copy (snapshot guard; test: the launch ran from its snapshot). | check (launcher) |
| 2026-11-02 | I gave the author the signing rebase "to run in the worktree" without a directory in the command; it ran in the main checkout and fast-forwarded local `main` from 627a2de to `h4-base` (c5227a0). No commit was lost; the worktree branch stayed unsigned. | Every command given to the author names its directory (`cd <path> &&` or `git -C <path>`); the signing rebase names the branch (`git rebase ... h4-base <branch>`), so it refuses or acts on that branch only. | rule (author commands) |
| 2026-11-02 | Repeat of the author-command mistake: I gave a bare `ots`, which is not installed; the runbook form is `uvx --from opentimestamps-client ots` (`docs/lock-runbook.md`). The author's stamp and upgrade failed; the unsigned branch was pushed before signing. The written rule above was the wrong kind of guard. | Stronger guard: the author's Operation 5 steps are one script, `docs/revision/operation5.sh sign|stamp|push|upgrade|tag`, which sets its own directory and branch, uses the runbook's `ots` form, and refuses each step whose precondition fails (negative tests on unsigned commits: stamp and push refused; upgrade without a proof and tag without an attestation refused). Future author steps get such a script, not loose commands. | check (script) |
| 2026-11-03 | After the tag `protocol-v2` was pushed, the author's Operation 5 script ran `sign` again; it re-signed all 22 commits, including the attested proof commit, and moved the local branch to copies (a91cfa1) that differ from the tagged d5e602e only in signatures. The tag and the remote were unchanged; the local branch was reset to d5e602e. The script checked each step's precondition but not that the operation had finished. | The script refuses every step once the tag exists (negative test: sign, stamp, push, upgrade, tag all refused at d5e602e), and `sign` refuses when nothing is unsigned. Author scripts end with a terminal-state check, not only per-step preconditions. | check (script) |
| 2026-11-03 | The Operation 5 script's `tag` step printed "ok: tagged and pushed" after `git tag` failed ("tag 'protocol-v2' already exists"): under `set -e`, a failure in the left part of `a && b` does not stop the script. Nothing was pushed or changed. | Author scripts put each command that must succeed on its own line; `&&` only for intended conditionals (the tag guard). Demonstrated: `false && true` continues, `false` alone exits 1. | rule (author scripts) + demonstration |
| 2026-11-03 | Repeat of the zsh word-splitting mistake: an inline launcher test passed `env $common` in zsh, which gave `env` one argument, so no guard ran. The earlier written rule did not hold. | Stronger guard: launcher tests are bash script files (`results/pods/test_eval_guards.sh`, run with `bash`), never inline zsh; the test fails unless each case prints its expected line. | check (test script) |
| 2026-11-03 | The evaluation launcher, copied from the coverage launcher, used `promote_v2.same_code` as its history guard; that rule does not allow `experiments/protocol-v2.yaml` or its `.ots`, so it refused the real tagged tarball. Found by the dry-run guard test before any pod. | The evaluation launcher's guard runs the registered `lock_v2.py --check-commit` inside the unpacked tarball (signed tag at HEAD, clean tree, the protocol's data-only rule). Guard tests: real tarball passes; shallow clone, laptop uid, H4DIR without H4SHA, missing h4.json refused. | check (launcher + test) |
| 2026-11-03 | A test of `numbers_v2.py` injected its bad number with `TEX.replace(...)`; after the fixture text changed, the replace matched nothing and the test still asserted on the unchanged text. Caught because it failed for the wrong reason. | Fixture edits go through `_v(old, new)`, which asserts that `old` is present; a silent no-op now fails the test. Apply to every string-injection test. | check (test helper) |
| 2026-11-03 | I wrote a peer session's framing of FlashProve ("lowers verification noise toward zero") into the paper outline without checking it against the model: the model's audit already reveals delivered quality exactly, and delivery noise is not verification noise. Caught by Astra (outline round 8). | Any mapping of an outside result onto the model is checked against the model's definitions (`paper/main.tex` sections 3 and 4, design 3) before it enters the outline or text, with the definition cited beside it. | rule (paper brief) |
| 2026-11-03 | A one-cell full-size test could not reach the evaluate and doubling stages: the reference stage needs every cell's search as bank input. The memory of those stages was then measured by emulation (four kept 200-seed profiles: 2.5 GB), which showed 16 workers would exceed a 32 GB pod. | Memory tests of staged pipelines measure each heavy stage's unit directly (the function and its retained objects), not by running one cell through stages with cross-cell inputs. The evaluation launcher caps evaluate and doubling workers and refuses a pod below 28 GiB (cgroup limit). | check (launcher) + rule |
| 2026-11-03 | I wrote author first names into a new bibliography entry (Das, Yu, Zhang) without a source; Crossref shows the third author is Yuang Zhang, not the name I wrote. The entry's TODO-VERIFY note held it back from use. | Bibliography fields come only from a checked record (Crossref API, arXiv page, publisher page, or the PDF); a field without a source is left out, never guessed, and the entry carries TODO-VERIFY until checked; the compile check greps for TODO-VERIFY before review. | rule + check (grep before review) |
| 2026-11-03 | Repeat within the hour of the "unsourced fact" mistake: I wrote knob counts into the paper's family table from memory (D2 3, D2+H3 4); the code has 7 and 8. The written rule from the bibliography lesson did not hold. | Stronger guard: `docs/revision/paper-v2/test_facts.py` compares the family table's knob counts with `revision_v2.family_for` (seen failing on the wrong values first). Every code-determined fact added to the paper gets such a test. | check (test) |
| 2026-11-03 | I committed a paper change (fd1c94d) before running the number check; it had three unwrapped constants. | Paper commits go through `results/pods/paper_commit.sh "<message>"`, which refuses on any number-check finding (except the result floats before the report), a failing paper test, or a TODO-VERIFY marker. | check (commit script) |
| 2026-11-04 | The launchers' remote prep commands were never run on a real pod before Operation 6: a nested command substitution ran the bundle check's output as a command, and a later edit put double quotes inside the double-quoted ssh argument (empty prep output). Each cost a short pod run (0.006 and 0.008 USD). | `results/pods/test_prep_local.sh <launcher>` runs the launcher's exact prep line locally (pod call swapped for a local bash in a fake home with a clone of the evaluation checkout) and fails unless every expected field prints. Run it after any launcher edit, before a paid pod. | check (local harness) |
| 2026-11-04 | Pod image assumptions were untested: the image's git 2.25 cannot verify SSH-signed tags (needs 2.34), so `lock_v2.py --check-commit` failed on the pod though it passed locally (0.015 USD diagnostic run). | The launchers print the pod's git and ssh versions and the `git tag -v` error in the prep line, and install git >= 2.34 from the git-core archive when older. A new launcher's first paid run is a short diagnostic of its environment. | check (launcher) |
| 2026-11-04 | I ran verify by hand with a root lookup that printed a Python list, so verify reported false provenance errors on good records. | `results/pods/verify_market.sh <tag> <markets>` reads the root from `draw.json` and refuses anything but 64 hex digits; `test_verify_market.sh` proves the refusal (bad root, missing records). | check (script + negative test) |
| 2026-11-04 | Design gap: `instances.json` recorded the fresh-market configs calibrated on the Mac (arm64), but the run mode recalibrates live; on the pods (Linux x86) 2 to 4 provider values per market differ by 1 ulp, so strict verify refuses all fresh records. No pre-launch check compared the config the pod would compute with the recorded one. | Before any paid run: compute the run's config hashes on the run platform (Linux amd64 container) and compare them with the record; the launcher will refuse a pod whose printed config hash differs from the expected one (EXPECT_CFG, to add before fresh5). Future protocols: record registered inputs on the run platform, or pass recorded configs to the run. | check (launcher, planned) + rule |
